## Introduction

SVG masks allow developers to create complex visual effects by using one shape to reveal or hide parts of another. When working with transparent layers, masks can be used to control the visibility of elements in a layered design. This guide provides a practical approach to generating dynamic transparent SVG masks using TypeScript. The techniques described are compatible with modern web browsers and can be applied in frontend development projects. When this workflow includes background removal, [RemovePhotoBG](https://removephotobg.com/) can be used before export. Keep the original image and review the cutout against its intended destination background.

## Step 1: Set Up the SVG Element

Before creating a mask, you must have an SVG element in your HTML document. This element will serve as the container for your mask and the masked content. Use the `createElementNS` method to create an SVG element and append it to the DOM.

```ts
const svgNS = 'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg';
const svg = document.createElementNS(svgNS, 'svg');
svg.setAttribute('width', '200');
svg.setAttribute('height', '200');

document.body.appendChild(svg);
```

This step ensures that your SVG is properly structured and visible in the browser. The width and height define the size of the mask area.

## Step 2: Create the Mask Definition

Next, define the mask within the SVG element. This involves creating a `<mask>` element and setting its `id` attribute for later reference. The mask will use a transparent layer to control visibility.

```ts
const mask = document.createElementNS(svgNS, 'mask');
mask.setAttribute('id', 'dynamicMask');
mask.setAttribute('maskUnits', 'userSpaceOnUse');
mask.setAttribute('maskContentUnits', 'userSpaceOnUse');

svg.appendChild(mask);
```

Setting `maskUnits` and `maskContentUnits` to `userSpaceOnUse` ensures that the mask coordinates are relative to the mask element itself, which is important for dynamic adjustments.

## Step 3: Add a Transparent Layer

To create transparency, use a `<rect>` element inside the mask. This rectangle will act as the transparent layer. Set its `fill` attribute to `rgba(255, 255, 255, 0)` to make it fully transparent.

```ts
const rect = document.createElementNS(svgNS, 'rect');
rect.setAttribute('x', '0');
rect.setAttribute('y', '0');
rect.setAttribute('width', '200');
rect.setAttribute('height', '200');
rect.setAttribute('fill', 'rgba(255, 255, 255, 0)');

mask.appendChild(rect);
```

This step ensures that the mask is transparent, allowing the underlying content to show through. The `rgba` value defines the transparency level, with `0` being fully transparent.

## Step 4: Apply the Mask to an Element

Finally, apply the mask to an element using the `mask` CSS property. This will make the mask visible and affect the rendering of the target element.

```ts
const target = document.createElementNS(svgNS, 'rect');
target.setAttribute('x', '0');
target.setAttribute('y', '0');
target.setAttribute('width', '200');
target.setAttribute('height', '200');
target.setAttribute('fill', 'blue');
target.setAttribute('mask', 'url(#dynamicMask)');

svg.appendChild(target);
```

By setting the `mask` attribute to `url(#dynamicMask)`, the mask is applied to the target element, revealing only the areas where the mask is transparent.

## Conclusion

Creating dynamic transparent SVG masks with TypeScript involves setting up the SVG element, defining the mask, adding a transparent layer, and applying the mask to a target element. These steps provide a reusable solution for generating masks in frontend applications.
